    <string name="note_1_title">12, 24 AND 48 VOLT LIMITS</string>
    <string name="note_1_body">This tool covers low-voltage DC only. At 12 V a small drop costs a large share of the supply, so keep the loss cap at 3 percent for lighting and 5 percent for motors and heaters.</string>
    <string name="note_2_title">A FUSE PROTECTS THE CABLE</string>
    <string name="note_2_body">Size the fuse to the wire, not to the load. Pick the smallest standard rating above the working current that still sits at or below the ampacity of the chosen wire.</string>
    <string name="note_3_title">COPPER VS ALUMINIUM</string>
    <string name="note_3_body">Aluminium carries about 61 percent of the current of copper for the same area, so an aluminium run needs roughly two sizes more to reach the same drop and the same ampacity.</string>
    <string name="note_4_title">BUNDLING AND AMBIENT HEAT</string>
    <string name="note_4_body">Cables bundled in a conduit or run through a warm engine bay lose ampacity. Derate the table by one size when several loaded conductors share a sleeve.</string>
    <string name="note_5_title">GARDEN AND OUTDOOR RUNS</string>
    <string name="note_5_body">Use cable rated for burial or UV exposure, seal every joint in a gland or a filled enclosure, and keep connections above the water line rather than in a buried box.</string>
    <string name="note_6_title">WHEN TO CALL A PROFESSIONAL</string>
    <string name="note_6_body">Anything on mains voltage, any fixed wiring in a building and any work behind a consumer unit belongs to a qualified electrician. This tool is for low-voltage DC runs only.</string>
